<ReviewLayout
  title="The pull request reviewer"
  description="efferent-review reads a pull request, its diff, the repository's rules and its CI, and reviews it with a model in a host's loop on the Efferent kernel: a small core of plain code, and a plugin behind every port."
  current=""
  headings={headings}
>
  <h2 id="what-it-does">What it does</h2>
  <p>
    <code>@xandreed/review</code> is a package of this repository whose program is <code>efferent-review</code>. It
    is private: it is gated and tested like every package, but it is not on npm. The program has two commands:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>efferent-review prepare &lt;number&gt; --repo &lt;owner/name&gt;</code> fetches the pull request and
      prints what a review would be given, asking no model.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>efferent-review review &lt;number&gt; --repo &lt;owner/name&gt;</code> prepares it, runs the model's turns
      over it, merges what they find, has the model check the findings again, and prints the report.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    It reads the pull request through <code>gh</code> and its code through <code>git</code>. It posts nothing: the
    forge it reads is read-only, and the report goes to the terminal (or as JSON with <code>--json</code>). Asked
    with <code>--run</code> or <code>--validate</code>, it also runs the pull request's own commands, each in a
    bubblewrap sandbox.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-pipeline">The pipeline</h2>
  <p>
    A review is six stages. Four are plain code, each a pure function with table tests or an adapter behind a port.
    Two are a model's <strong>turns</strong>: one turn reviews a group of changed files, and one turn checks one
    finding. Every turn is the host's own loop on the kernel, made of <strong>steps</strong>, one model call each.
  </p>
  <PipelineDiagram />

  <h2 id="words">The words it uses</h2>
  <div class="rv-cards not-prose">
    <div class="rv-card"><h3>Turn</h3><p>One run of the model's loop, in a session of its own: a finder turn reviews a group of files, a check reviews one finding.</p></div>
    <div class="rv-card"><h3>Step</h3><p>One model call inside a turn, and the tool calls its answer asks for. A turn stops at its step cap.</p></div>
    <div class="rv-card"><h3>Finding</h3><p>What a finder turn reports with <code>reportFinding</code>: a file, its lines, a kind, a severity, a claim and the quoted evidence.</p></div>
    <div class="rv-card"><h3>Bucket</h3><p>Findings at the same place in one file, merged by code. A bucket is checked, and reported once.</p></div>
    <div class="rv-card"><h3>Check and verdict</h3><p>A turn that tries to refute one finding and answers with <code>verdict</code>: confirmed, refuted or unsure, with a quote.</p></div>
    <div class="rv-card"><h3>Port and plugin</h3><p>A port is a contract the core calls; a plugin is the layer that fulfils it. Every plugin can be swapped.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="read-on">Read on</h2>
  <div class="rv-cards not-prose">
    <a class="rv-card" href={reviewHref("pipeline")}><h3>The pipeline</h3><p>Each stage in detail: what is fetched, how the diff is packed and grouped, the tools, merging, checking, the report and the records.</p></a>
    <a class="rv-card" href={reviewHref("loop")}><h3>The loop inside a turn</h3><p>Steps, tool calls, the step cap and the closing call, the spend ledgers, and why the history is append-only.</p></a>
    <a class="rv-card" href={reviewHref("plugins")}><h3>Core and plugins</h3><p>The small core, every port and its stock adapter, and what a host can swap, with code from the package.</p></a>
    <a class="rv-card" href={reviewHref("safety")}><h3>Safety</h3><p>Why trimming the environment is no isolation, the bubblewrap sandbox, untrusted text framed, rules read from the base.</p></a>
    <a class="rv-card" href={reviewHref("planned")}><h3>Planned</h3><p>What is not built yet: memory tied to the codebase, language-server tools, review skills by path, preloaded context.</p></a>
    <a class="rv-card" href={href("/learn")}><h3>The kernel, lesson by lesson</h3><p>The reviewer's turns run on the same kernel the learn track builds: <code>modelAnswer</code>, <code>callTool</code>, sessions and journals.</p></a>
  </div>
</ReviewLayout>
